## Experiment
Topic: Topic On a runner where `docker info`, `docker version`, and `docker ps` pass, do all six primary and six replay executions complete within the 1,200-second timed section? _(auto-enqueued follow-up from topic-can-make-reproduce-be-run-from-a-fresh-iso)_
Goal: Run six deterministic orphan-work container fixtures and six matched clean controls twice, comparing parent-exit and process-tree-aware completion decisions within a 1,200-second timed section.

Protocol to implement:
1. Use Python 3.11 or newer on the host and install only matplotlib with `pip install matplotlib==3.8.4`; use the Python standard library for all other host and container logic. Before timing, require `docker info`, `docker version`, and `docker ps` to return exit code 0. Use a locally available `python:3.12-slim` image and prohibit pulling during the experiment; if `docker image inspect python:3.12-slim` fails, report the run as an infrastructure failure rather than a hypothesis result. Build a local image named `completion-monitor-test:1` containing only the fixture scripts. Run every container with `--network none --cpus 1 --memory 256m --pids-limit 128` and a unique host temporary directory bind-mounted at `/trial`.
2. Implement a PID-1 Python supervisor. It starts the designated workload parent with `start_new_session=True`, records that parent's PID/process-group ID, reaps exited children, records the designated parent's exact exit code atomically in `/trial/parent_exit.json`, and remains alive until the host terminates the container. Every fixture process must remain in the designated parent's process group, including descendants reparented to PID 1.
3. Implement six fixture topologies: F1 `cpu-single`, one CPU-loop descendant; F2 `cpu-chain`, a depth-two descendant chain whose leaf performs the CPU loop; F3 `cpu-fanout`, three CPU-loop children; F4 `open-single`, one child holding `/trial/output_0.bin` open; F5 `open-chain`, a depth-two chain whose leaf holds `/trial/output_0.bin` open; and F6 `open-fanout`, two children holding `/trial/output_0.bin` and `/trial/output_1.bin`, respectively. Use seeds 101, 102, 103, 201, 202, and 203 for F1-F6. CPU workers repeatedly apply SHA-256 to `seed || worker_index || iteration` and poll for `/trial/release` every 10,000 iterations. Output workers deterministically write and flush 65,536 bytes generated from repeated SHA-256 blocks based on their seed and worker index, call `os.fsync`, and then retain the writable file descriptor while polling for `/trial/release` every 10 ms. Each descendant writes an atomic readiness file; the designated parent writes `/trial/all_ready` only after every required descendant is ready.
4. Give every fixture two variants. In the `dirty` variant, after `/trial/all_ready` exists, the designated parent exits with code 0 without creating `/trial/release` or waiting for descendants. In the matched `clean` variant, the parent creates `/trial/release`, waits for all direct children, with chain nodes recursively waiting for their own children, verifies that all expected result or output files exist, and then exits 0. The process graph, seeds, declared outputs, and worker code must otherwise be identical between variants.
5. Implement two host-side completion monitors evaluated at the same checkpoint. The naive monitor returns complete exactly when `/trial/parent_exit.json` exists and contains exit code 0. The tree-aware monitor returns complete only when all three conditions hold: the designated parent exited 0; a `/proc` scan inside the container finds zero non-zombie processes whose process-group ID equals the recorded group ID; and a scan of `/proc/[0-9]*/fd/*` finds no descriptor resolving to any declared output path. Have an in-container probe perform both `/proc` scans and emit JSON containing `live_group_member_pids`, `open_output_fds`, and the resulting Boolean. Treat probe errors as non-completion.
6. Before the timed section, perform one untimed smoke test using F1-clean and confirm that both monitors eventually return complete. Delete its files and container. Do not use smoke-test observations in metrics.
7. Start `time.monotonic()` for the 1,200-second timed section. Execute two passes named `primary` and `replay`. In each pass, run F1-F6, and for each fixture run its dirty variant followed by its clean variant, yielding 24 measured container runs: 6 fixtures x 2 variants x 2 passes. Use the same seed and configuration in primary and replay. Run sequentially to avoid resource-contention ambiguity.
8. For each run, start the container and wait at most 10 seconds for `/trial/parent_exit.json`. Within 250 ms of observing that file, evaluate and record both monitors before creating any release file from the host. This is the checkpoint decision. Also record parent exit code, live process-group member count, open declared-output descriptor count, checkpoint latency from parent-exit observation, and elapsed wall time. A timeout, nonzero parent exit, missing readiness state, probe failure, or unexpected container exit is a failed run.
9. After the checkpoint evaluation of a dirty run, atomically create `/trial/release`. Poll the tree-aware monitor every 50 ms for at most 10 seconds and record time to eventual acceptance. Require all descendants to disappear, all declared outputs to close, and deterministic result/output files to be finalized before stopping the container. For clean runs, the tree-aware monitor must already accept at the checkpoint. Stop and remove each container before beginning the next run.
10. Compute SHA-256 digests of every finalized result and declared output. For each fixture and variant, require the primary and replay digest vectors to match exactly. Store one CSV row per run and a JSON summary containing all decisions, counts, timings, digests, failures, and total timed-section duration.
11. Produce `completion_rates.png` with two panels, one for primary and one for replay. In each panel plot four bars showing checkpoint completion rate over the six fixtures for `dirty/naive`, `dirty/tree-aware`, `clean/naive`, and `clean/tree-aware`; use a y-axis from 0 to 1 and annotate each bar with its accepted-run count out of 6.
12. End the timed section only after all 24 runs, metrics, CSV/JSON output, and PNG creation are complete. The hypothesis is supported only if the timed duration is at most 1,200 seconds, there are no failed runs or digest mismatches, and all exact decision thresholds in `expected_if_true` are met.

Metrics to compute: Dirty naive checkpoint acceptance rate: number of dirty runs accepted by the naive monitor divided by 12; compute separately by pass over 6 runs as well., Dirty tree-aware checkpoint acceptance rate: number of dirty runs accepted by the tree-aware monitor divided by 12; compute separately by pass., Clean naive and tree-aware checkpoint acceptance rates: accepted clean runs divided by 12 for each monitor., Paired dirty monitor gap: mean over the 12 dirty runs of `naive_complete - tree_aware_complete`; support threshold is at least 0.99, with the required exact result 1.00., Dirty checkpoint descendant evidence: live process-group member count for every dirty run; required minimum is 1., Open-output evidence: open declared-output descriptor count for F4-F6 dirty runs; required minimum is 1, while F1-F3 must be rejected because of live descendants even if this count is 0., Eventual tree-aware acceptance: fraction of dirty runs accepted within 10 seconds after release; required value is 12/12., Replay determinism rate: fraction of fixture/variant primary-replay pairs with identical finalized SHA-256 digest vectors; required value is 12/12., Timed-section duration: monotonic seconds covering all 24 measured runs and artifact generation; required maximum is 1,200 seconds., Execution success count: measured runs with no timeout, probe error, nonzero parent exit, premature container exit, or cleanup failure; required value is 24/24.
Expected if hypothesis holds: Both primary and replay must show dirty/naive = 6/6, dirty/tree-aware = 0/6, clean/naive = 6/6, and clean/tree-aware = 6/6 at the checkpoint. Across passes, the dirty paired monitor gap must equal 1.00; every dirty run must have at least one live process-group member, every dirty F4-F6 run must have at least one open declared-output descriptor, all 12 dirty runs must become tree-aware-complete within 10 seconds after release, all 12 primary/replay digest pairs must match, all 24 runs must succeed, and the timed section must finish in at most 1,200 seconds.
Expected if hypothesis fails: The hypothesis is refuted if any dirty run is not accepted by the naive monitor after a recorded parent exit of 0, any dirty run is accepted by the tree-aware monitor while a descendant or declared-output descriptor remains, either monitor rejects any clean run, the dirty paired monitor gap is below 0.99, or a primary/replay decision differs. The operational 12-execution timing claim is also refuted if the measured section exceeds 1,200 seconds or any measured execution fails; a missing local base image is reported separately as infrastructure failure.
Wall-clock budget: about 25 minutes. Keep the experiment inside it; if
something is slow, reduce sample sizes rather than exceeding the budget.

